    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8u014bd",
      "content": "Biological classification is the scientific arrangement of organisms into groups based on similarities and differences. It helps in identification, naming and understanding evolutionary relationships among organisms."
    },
    {
      "id": "rb22wxm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Basis of Classification"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "a2kyjju",
      "content": "• Cell type (Prokaryotic/Eukaryotic)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "34987bu",
      "content": "• Cell wall composition"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2h60zld",
      "content": "• Mode of nutrition (Autotrophic/Heterotrophic)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vkc4sp5",
      "content": "• Body organization"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hi0h0o8",
      "content": "• Reproduction method"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ajg6f47",
      "content": "• Phylogenetic relationships"
    },
    {
      "id": "yhwzhwg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Two Kingdom & Five Kingdom Concepts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k4wzo2t",
      "content": "Earlier classification included Two Kingdom system (Plantae and Animalia). R.H. Whittaker proposed Five Kingdom Classification in 1969 based on cell structure, body organization and nutrition."
    },
    {
      "id": "a3yuvck",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Five Kingdom Classification Overview"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m811hqp",
      "content": "The five kingdoms are Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia."
    },
